== Preface

#smallcaps[Christian Reader],

#smallcaps[I will] tell you in a few words the reasons that won my consent to publish the discourse that follows. The chief place among them belongs to the present state and condition of most of those who profess the faith. The visible evidence of the state of their hearts and spirits shows a great inability to deal with the temptations that surround them, both from the peace they have in the world and from the divisions they have among themselves. I am sure this is of such great importance that if this book only moves others to press on men’s consciences more effectually the work of considering their ways, and to give clearer direction for reaching the end proposed, I will think well of my part in this undertaking. A second reason was the dangerous mistakes I have observed in some men who have lately taken it upon themselves to give directions for the mortification of sin. Being unacquainted with the mystery of the gospel and the efficacy of the death of Christ, they have once again laid the yoke of a self-made mortification on the necks of their disciples, a yoke which neither they nor their forefathers were ever able to bear. The mortification they cry up and press on others matches that of the gospel neither in its nature, its subject, its causes, its means, nor its effects. It constantly produces the sad results of superstition, self-righteousness, and anxiety of conscience in those who take up the burden that is so bound on them.

What is set out here in weakness will, I humbly hope, agree with the spirit and the letter of the gospel, and with the experience of those who know what it is to walk with God according to the terms of the covenant of grace. So if not this, then certainly something of this kind is very necessary at this time, to promote and further this work of gospel mortification in the hearts of believers, and to direct them in paths that are safe, where they may find rest for their souls. I have something to add about what concerns me in particular. I preached on this subject with some encouraging success, through the grace of Him who supplies seed to the sower. Several people in whose hearts are the ways of God then pressed me to publish what I had preached, with such additions and changes as I judged necessary. While their desires urged me on, I remembered a debt in which I have now stood for some years to several noble and worthy Christian friends: a treatise on Communion with God, promised to them some time ago.#footnote[Since the first edition of this treatise, that other one has also been published.] So I thought that if I could not pay off the greater debt with this, I might perhaps offer them this discourse of #emph[being at odds with themselves] as interest for their patience over the one on #emph[peace and communion with God]. Besides this, I considered that providence had engaged me in the public debate of several controversies in religion, and that this might seem to call for something of another, more generally useful kind, the fruit of choice and not of necessity. For these and similar reasons this short discourse is brought out to public view, and now presented to you. I hope I may honestly say that my heart’s desire to God, and the chief aim of my life in the place where the good providence of God has set me, is this: that mortification and universal holiness may be promoted in my own heart and in the hearts and ways of others, to the glory of God, so that the gospel of our Lord and Savior Jesus Christ may be adorned in all things. To that end, if this little discourse (and this is the whole account I will give of why it is published) may in any way be useful to the least of the saints, it will be regarded as an answer to the weak prayers with which it is sent out by its unworthy author,

#smallcaps[John Owen].
